3. Execution boundaries
- Read-only audit. No gateway restart, service restart, certificate change, endpoint change, trial reset, new connection, tag write, UDT edit, graphic edit, database write, batch action, or historian configuration change.
- Do not disable, revert, temporarily replace, or otherwise disturb any working live mapping established in prior commissions.
- Preserve FAT-tested UDT definitions, instance paths, graphic bindings, scaling, engineering units, historian settings and batch/reporting behavior.
- Do not issue OPC writes, including ConnectSystemTag, DisconnectTag, setpoints or instrument commands. Do not start or stop equipment or processes.
- Use existing authorized gateway access and existing connected OPC sessions. Do not enable disabled connections for this audit. No broad network/port scans. Any additional host access or tests requiring authorization shall be proposed separately.
- Use bounded read-only browsing and reads; avoid high-frequency polling, recursive namespace dumps without limits, or load on active equipment. Do not capture credentials, private keys or tokens in deliverables.
- Do not fabricate equipment identities or substitute simulated values for evidence. Record inaccessible, offline or unverified equipment as such.
4. Execution steps
4.1 Capture current gateway state
Export or inspect through supported read-only mechanisms the current OPC client connection metadata and relevant tag configuration. Record gateway identity, timestamp, connection name, actual configured endpoint, enabled state, connection state, security policy/mode, and available server application identity. Redact secrets. Inventory all connections, including disabled legacy/test aliases. Identify MFCS_OPCUA, Unicorn-2 and Unicorn-4 specifically. Distinguish configured address from server-advertised address and workstation hostname. Do not infer that a disabled alias represents a different physical machine.
4.2 Establish vendor workstation and instrument inventory
For each connected UNICORN server, browse DataAccess/ Instruments and record exact instrument object names and stable node identifiers (namespace URI plus identifier where available). Identify model, serial number, system name, system connection status and equipment asset number from existing readable metadata, vendor configuration or authorized site evidence. If unavailable, create a specific site verification question. A hostname such as 815-avant150-01 is corroborating evidence only; it is not proof of actual instrument model or location.

For each connected MFCS server, inventory the exposed equipment identifiers, including both vessels of any DCU-TWIN. Determine which physical controllers and vessels are served by each workstation. Explicitly investigate .50 and .65 without assuming that an MFCS workstation is itself a bioreactor. Determine whether the active MFCS_OPCUA connection serves one or multiple physical rooms. Record actual endpoints, not the historical DA tunnel design if the deployment now uses UA.

Distinguish workstation IP, controller/instrument IP, vendor system ID and OPC endpoint. Multiple instruments may share one vendor workstation. Multiple OPC aliases may point to the same endpoint.
4.3 Trace existing Ignition equipment bindings
Enumerate the current equipment UDT instances and enabled state. Focus on Rooms 814 and 815, but include every live MFCS and UNICORN instance across the facility. For each live process parameter, resolve the effective OPC server name and OPC item path after UDT parameter substitution and instance overrides. Identify value source (OPC, memory/simulation, expression, derived or other), scaling, engineering units and available read quality/timestamp. Include Flow and Cond and all other mapped process parameters. Preserve the existing mapping exactly during this commission.

Trace each UDT to the Level 2 graphic, room/suite label, equipment identity, and batch setup/reporting configuration. Where available, inspect the historian measurement/tag identity and reporting association without modifying or generating records. Determine whether the previously commissioned left/right twins and UNICORN graphics are sourcing the correct physical equipment. Pay particular attention to cross-room mappings and duplicate instrument bindings. Do not assume a shared workstation means a shared instrument.
4.4 Cross-reference evidence and validate identity
Compare actual gateway configuration and vendor identity evidence against the original design inventory and physical site evidence. Maintain separate columns for design and observed values. Use a hierarchy of evidence: site-verified physical asset identity/room and vendor system configuration; live endpoint and stable instrument identity; effective Ignition bindings; customer design documentation; hostname or naming inference. Resolve conflicts only when evidence supports the conclusion. If site evidence and vendor metadata conflict, mark unresolved and request confirmation.

For each connected instrument, attempt a bounded read of representative process parameters using existing authorized connections. Record value, unit, quality and timestamp when available. GOOD quality alone is not an identity check. Idle/offline equipment may return zero, cached or unavailable data. Do not force a process run. Where a physical instrument cannot be confirmed, classify the mapping as unverified rather than aligned.
4.5 Classify discrepancies
Use statuses: ALIGNED, CONFIRMED_MISMATCH, DOCUMENTATION_MISMATCH, UNVERIFIED_IDENTITY, OFFLINE_OR_UNAVAILABLE, NOT_MAPPED, and NOT_APPLICABLE. Allow multiple findings per equipment. Identify separately whether the discrepancy is in workstation address, device role, physical room, model, instrument identity, UDT source, graphic association, batch/reporting association, or historian identity. Distinguish documentation-only corrections from changes requiring live configuration remediation.

If a probable wrong-room live mapping is found, flag it prominently for owner review, including potential impact on displayed process values and existing historical/batch data. Do not automatically disable it or rewrite historical records. Recommend any required operational restriction to the owner for approval.
